JF EXTREME vs its own exact L1 null: 0/11 (none).

- P(J1 | L0) = 8!/64! ~= 3.178e-85 (analytic, frozen section 6 -- the J1 space has exactly 8! members; 0 sampled hits expected at any feasible N).
- P(J2^J3 | J1) = 1/40,320 (exact count = the canonical palace order; residual 0 bits)
- P(J4 | J1) = 384/40,320 = 0.9524% (complement-respecting assignments; below the 1% EXTREME line)
- JF comp-sum (obs f) = 1024 (the exact-space maximum), reached by 9216/40,320 = 22.8571% of assignments; mid-percentile 88.57 of the exact J1 distribution.
  CORRECTED FC-4 ANCHOR (design Amendment 1, 2026-07-12): comp-sum 1024 is NOT rare under the exact J1 null -- the whole block-distance-maximizing class (4! x 4! x 2^4 = 9,216 assignments, ~22.86%) attains it, so it is expected NOT to flag EXTREME here. The original frozen FC-4 clause ('>=99th percentile, since 384/40,320 reach it') conflated 'reaches comp-sum 1024' with 'satisfies J4' (J4 is a strict subset). The palace ORDER is what is rare (the predicate rates above); the comp-sum scalar is a coarse proxy the whole maximizing class achieves.

